# Output

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

An output shows the result of a choice or a calculation, next to the controls that produced it. The usual case is the number a slider is set to. A slider on its own gives no written value, so an output beside it tells people what they have chosen.

This page owns the `<output>` element. For slider behaviour and the exact-value alternative, see [Control Slider](sliders.html).

This page covers:

- Output
  - `<output>`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

A label that names what the value is, the value and its unit. The output sits beside or just under the control, and keeps the same place as the value changes.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-output"><code>&lt;output&gt;</code></h3>

The browser’s element for a calculated or chosen result. It holds text and inline content, and can be named with a `<label for>`.

- `for` lists the ids of the controls that fed the result, separated by spaces.
- `form` names the form it belongs to, and `name` names it within that form.
- It has the role `status`, which is a polite live region. When its text changes, a screen reader may announce it without moving focus. Support varies between browsers and screen readers.
- A form does not submit it. It is not a field.
- Resetting the form restores its starting text.

```html
<label for="brightness">Brightness</label>
<input id="brightness" type="range" min="0" max="100" value="40">
<p>Chosen brightness: <output for="brightness" aria-live="off">40%</output></p>
```

When the value changes as someone drags, set `aria-live="off"`, as above, so that every step is not announced. For the exact value and what to do after a change, see [Continuous controls and alternatives](../home-assistant/continuous-controls.html).

Home Assistant provides no output element, and a dashboard author can write one only in HTML they are allowed to write, such as a custom card. The Civica theme has no output variable. The text takes the colour of the text around it, so it follows `primary-text-color` and `secondary-text-color`. Write the unit every time.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It joins a value to the controls that produced it.
- The value is written as text, so it is never only a picture or a colour.
- Announcements depend on the screen reader and the browser.
- It does not replace a status message or an exact-value field.
- It has no border or background, so its text must be readable by itself.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Show the unit, keep the output in a steady place beside the control, and do not update it faster than people can read. Give a drag-only control an exact-value alternative. Do not use an output for a general status line, and do not carry a value by colour alone. Updating an output must never change the context, such as moving focus or submitting.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: `for` and a label link the value to its control.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour and 1.4.3 Contrast (Minimum): the value is text, and readable on its own.
- 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide: avoid a value that updates continuously where people must read it.
- 3.2.2 On Input: changing the value does not change the page.
- 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions: the label says what the value is.
- 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value and 4.1.3 Status Messages: the role is status, and `aria-live="off"` stops announcements while dragging.

